WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.211 --> 00:00:14.089
<v Daniel>Imagine it's the year 2000. The internet boom is in full swing, tech is absolutely everywhere, and you would probably bet your life savings that raw, introverted brain power is about to become the most valuable currency on earth.</v>

2
00:00:14.738 --> 00:00:16.839
<v Maya>I mean, it just makes intuitive sense, right?</v>

3
00:00:16.999 --> 00:00:31.423
<v Daniel>Right. It really does. You add highly complex technology to the economy, so the demand for analytical, math-heavy cognitive skill should just go straight up, like a clean, linear equation. But you would be completely wrong.</v>

4
00:00:32.003 --> 00:00:38.325
<v Maya>It is baffling when you actually look at the data from that era, because that linear equation completely broke down.</v>

5
00:00:38.943 --> 00:00:51.699
<v Daniel>Okay, let's unpack this. Our mission today is to explore a stack of sources that completely redefine the hidden engine of the modern labor market. And we are going to look at how artificial intelligence is forcing us to rethink all of it.</v>

6
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.641
<v Maya>Yeah, it's quite the paradigm shift.</v>

7
00:00:53.841 --> 00:01:08.568
<v Daniel>It is. This is exactly the kind of deep dive you need if you're trying to synthesize the real evidence on where work is actually heading. So we start with this massive economic puzzle from the early 2000s. If technology inherently favors high skill, why did the</v>

8
00:01:08.708 --> 00:01:14.130
<v Daniel>economic returns to cognitive skills suddenly just, well, flatline after the year 2000?</v>

9
00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:20.233
<v Maya>And the mystery goes even deeper than just wages. We actually have to look at the actual jobs people were doing.</v>

10
00:01:20.594 --> 00:01:31.071
<v Daniel>Right. Why did STEM occupations, science, technology, engineering, and math, why did they actually shrink by 0.12 percentage points of the total labor force between 2000 and 2012? Which</v>

11
00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:32.734
<v Maya>is wild when you think about it.</v>

12
00:01:33.197 --> 00:01:47.906
<v Daniel>It is. Keep in mind, this was after STEM had grown by 1.33 points over the previous 20 years. The tech revolution is accelerating, yet STEM jobs are shrinking as a share of the pie. Meanwhile, other cognitive heavy jobs,</v>

13
00:01:48.006 --> 00:01:54.310
<v Daniel>think managers, nurses, physicians, lawyers, they grew by 2.87 points in that exact same window.</v>

14
00:01:55.053 --> 00:02:03.258
<v Maya>This raises an important question. What was happening in the labor market that suddenly penalized brilliant people who worked entirely alone?</v>

15
00:02:03.538 --> 00:02:03.919
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

16
00:02:04.179 --> 00:02:19.168
<v Maya>The answer to that puzzle is anchored in one specific landmark 2017 paper by the economist David Deming, published in the Quarterly Journal of Economics. Deming realized that the nature of work had shifted underneath our feet. Being exceptionally smart simply wasn't enough</v>

17
00:02:19.208 --> 00:02:23.991
<v Maya>anymore. The premium had shifted to how efficiently workers could coordinate.</v>

18
00:02:24.518 --> 00:02:34.686
<v Daniel>But okay, before we go further, we have to define social skill. Because if you're listening to this, you are probably picturing the friendly, warm person at the water cooler.</v>

19
00:02:34.826 --> 00:02:36.007
<v Maya>Right, the office cheerleader.</v>

20
00:02:36.127 --> 00:02:42.492
<v Daniel>Yeah, the person who brings donuts and remembers everyone's dog's name. We are not talking about being extroverted or warm, are we?</v>

21
00:02:42.763 --> 00:02:57.434
<v Maya>We are absolutely not. And if we don't clarify that, the rest of this economic model won't make any sense. In Deming's framework, social skill has a very strict, almost mechanical definition. It is entirely about coordination costs.</v>

22
00:02:58.034 --> 00:02:59.075
<v Daniel>Coordination costs.</v>

23
00:02:59.535 --> 00:03:05.019
<v Maya>Yes. Deming models teamwork by adapting David Ricardo's classic economic theory of international trade.</v>

24
00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:10.003
<v Daniel>Wait, the 19th century theory where like Portugal trades wine for England's cloth?</v>

25
00:03:10.188 --> 00:03:17.497
<v Maya>That's the one. But instead of countries trading goods, Deming models a modern office as workers trading tasks.</v>

26
00:03:17.938 --> 00:03:23.505
<v Daniel>So how does trading tasks actually play out on, say, a Tuesday afternoon during a software sprint?</v>

27
00:03:24.268 --> 00:03:38.917
<v Maya>Well, think of a worker's pure cognitive skill as their average productivity. You know, how fast and accurately they can code or analyze or write. Their social skill, on the other hand, acts as a friction reducer. It</v>

28
00:03:38.977 --> 00:03:41.378
<v Maya>dictates the cost of trading tasks with colleagues.</v>

29
00:03:42.038 --> 00:03:42.759
<v Daniel>Okay, I'm following.</v>

30
00:03:43.111 --> 00:03:57.917
<v Maya>So imagine you have a brilliant software engineer, top of their class, but with a very low social skill. Communicating with them, getting them to accept feedback, or having them explain their architecture to the marketing team, it takes an enormous amount of time and</v>

31
00:03:57.957 --> 00:03:58.697
<v Maya>emotional energy.</v>

32
00:03:59.317 --> 00:04:03.059
<v Daniel>So they are essentially a country with terrible ports and massive tariffs.</v>

33
00:04:03.419 --> 00:04:04.740
<v Maya>Yes, perfect analogy.</v>

34
00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:10.522
<v Daniel>It is just too expensive to do business with them. So what happens? Does the company just fire them?</v>

35
00:04:11.693 --> 00:04:26.661
<v Maya>Usually no, but because they are so expensive to trade with, they end up doing too many tasks alone. They might be a genius coder, but because they can't hand things off efficiently, they're now also trying to do their own project management, their own client communication, their</v>

36
00:04:26.721 --> 00:04:27.901
<v Maya>own quality assurance.</v>

37
00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:29.222
<v Daniel>They become a bottleneck.</v>

38
00:04:29.442 --> 00:04:44.070
<v Maya>Exactly. They lose out on the massive benefits of specialization. Meanwhile, a team of slightly less brilliant coders who can seamlessly hand tasks back and forth, capitalizing on each other's comparative advantages, they will completely outproduce the isolated</v>

39
00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:44.471
<v Maya>genius.</v>

40
00:04:44.671 --> 00:04:53.195
<v Daniel>That makes total sense, especially when you consider the types of jobs that we're growing. This kind of task trading is inherently more valuable in non-routine jobs, right?</v>

41
00:04:53.416 --> 00:04:53.896
<v Maya>Definitely.</v>

42
00:04:54.354 --> 00:05:04.417
<v Daniel>Because if you are on an assembly line, the coordination is built into the conveyor belt. But if you are in a modern office, things are unpredictable. You constantly have to adjust on the fly.</v>

43
00:05:04.797 --> 00:05:12.620
<v Maya>And that relies on an underlying concept deeply explored by David Autor in his 2015 work on Polanyi's Paradox.</v>

44
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:13.820
<v Daniel>Oh, Polanyi's Paradox.</v>

45
00:05:14.067 --> 00:05:28.875
<v Maya>Yeah, the paradox is simply the idea that humans know more than we can tell. Historically, tasks that required tacit human interaction, reading a room, managing a delicate negotiation, interpreting a vague client request, they couldn't be</v>

46
00:05:28.935 --> 00:05:31.197
<v Maya>spelled out as explicit step-by-step rules.</v>

47
00:05:32.077 --> 00:05:38.681
<v Daniel>And if you can't write the rule, you can't write the code. So those tasks vigorously resisted computer automation.</v>

48
00:05:38.701 --> 00:05:53.661
<v Maya>Precisely. Computers swallowed up the routine, repetitive tasks. So what was left over for humans? The messy, interactive, non-routine tasks. And to navigate a whole economy of non-routine tasks, you need human beings who can trade tasks with low</v>

49
00:05:53.701 --> 00:05:54.101
<v Maya>friction.</v>

50
00:05:54.301 --> 00:06:03.504
<v Daniel>Wow. Let's look at the actual numbers Deming used to prove this. Because if you were trying to build an ironclad argument for how work has changed, these are the stats you want to quote.</v>

51
00:06:03.564 --> 00:06:04.905
<v Maya>The numbers are pretty undeniable.</v>

52
00:06:05.045 --> 00:06:17.098
<v Daniel>Yeah. When Deming tallied up job growth as a share of all jobs from 1980 to 2012, occupations that were intensive in social skills grew by a staggering 11.8 percentage points.</v>

53
00:06:17.358 --> 00:06:24.000
<v Maya>But the composition of that 11.8 percent is where the real story lives. It wasn't just social skills operating in a vacuum.</v>

54
00:06:24.402 --> 00:06:38.851
<v Daniel>No, it was a combination. Of that massive growth, 7.2 points came from jobs demanding both high math and high social skills. Another 4.6 points came from jobs demanding low math but high social skills. Right. But here is the most</v>

55
00:06:38.931 --> 00:06:50.018
<v Daniel>striking data point of all. Jobs requiring high math but low social skills didn't just stagnate. They actually shrank by about 3.3 points. They practically vanished from the growth charts.</v>

56
00:06:50.548 --> 00:07:05.243
<v Maya>And the financial markets responded accordingly. The wages followed that exact same trajectory. How so? Well, over that same 30 year period, wages grew by roughly 26 percent in those high math, high social jobs. Compare that to the high math,</v>

57
00:07:05.604 --> 00:07:12.371
<v Maya>low social jobs, which saw wage growth of just five point nine percent. The market started heavily penalizing the brilliant jerk.</v>

58
00:07:12.948 --> 00:07:27.312
<v Daniel>To really prove this wasn't just a broader macroeconomic fluke, Deming looked at a cohort shift. He used the National Longitudinal Survey of Youth, which, for those who don't spend their days in labor economics, is essentially the gold standard data set.</v>

59
00:07:27.452 --> 00:07:28.012
<v Maya>It really is.</v>

60
00:07:28.372 --> 00:07:43.275
<v Daniel>Yeah, it tracks the exact same individuals over decades to see how their lives and careers actually unfold. Debbie compared the 1979 cohort against the 1997 cohort, looking specifically at how they fared when they were between the ages of 25 and</v>

61
00:07:43.315 --> 00:07:43.835
<v Daniel>33.</v>

62
00:07:44.455 --> 00:07:58.798
<v Maya>It's the perfect way to isolate the changing value of a skill. The workplace of a 30-year-old in the late 1980s was fundamentally different from a 30-year-old in the late 2000s. We saw the rise of agile frameworks, cross-functional teams, constant digital communication.</v>

63
00:07:59.359 --> 00:08:01.319
<v Maya>The demand for coordination skyrocketed.</v>

64
00:08:01.892 --> 00:08:12.817
<v Daniel>And the data shows it. In the older 1979 cohort, if a worker's social skills rose by one standard deviation, their chance of securing full-time work only went up by 0.7 percentage points.</v>

65
00:08:13.277 --> 00:08:13.537
<v Maya>Right.</v>

66
00:08:14.317 --> 00:08:28.143
<v Daniel>But fast forward to the newer 1997 cohort, that exact same increase in social skills raised their chance of full-time work by 3.0 points. That is more than a four-fold increase in the value of social skills just to get your foot in the door.</v>

67
00:08:28.622 --> 00:08:35.464
<v Maya>And the wage return nearly doubled as well, rising from 2 percent to 3.7 percent for that same bump in social skills.</v>

68
00:08:35.944 --> 00:08:48.607
<v Daniel>We do need to add an essential caveat here just to paint the full picture. During that same generational shift, the wage return to cognitive skill actually fell, dropping from 20.3 percent down to 15.1 percent.</v>

69
00:08:51.060 --> 00:09:01.128
<v Maya>Yes, but we have to be very careful with how we interpret that drop. It does not mean cognitive skill is irrelevant. Far from it. A 15% return is still vastly larger than a 3.7% return in absolute terms.</v>

70
00:09:03.475 --> 00:09:05.635
<v Daniel>OK, so being smart still pays the bills.</v>

71
00:09:05.876 --> 00:09:20.558
<v Maya>It absolutely does. The headline we are looking at is the trajectory of the change. The ultimate takeaway, which is further backed up by Katherine Weinberger's 2014 research on earlier high school cohorts and a massive 2018 study by Deming and Kahn that</v>

72
00:09:20.638 --> 00:09:24.939
<v Maya>analyzed 45 million job ads, is complementarity.</v>

73
00:09:25.519 --> 00:09:26.340
<v Daniel>Complementarity.</v>

74
00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:33.141
<v Maya>Yes. Social and cognitive skills act as complements. The labor market pays a massive premium to people who possess both.</v>

75
00:09:33.709 --> 00:09:48.536
<v Daniel>Here's where it gets really interesting though. If you're listening to this, you might be rolling your eyes right now. Because you are probably wondering, how on earth did a survey that started in 1979 actually measure something as</v>

76
00:09:48.656 --> 00:09:56.821
<v Daniel>nuanced as lowering coordination costs? It's not like they had a standardized exam for empathy or cross-functional synergy.</v>

77
00:09:57.021 --> 00:10:08.306
<v Maya>They didn't. And we have to be completely honest about this vulnerability. The NLSY data sets have no real test for social skill. Deming had to build a proxy measure out of whatever he could find.</v>

78
00:10:08.727 --> 00:10:09.507
<v Daniel>So what did he use?</v>

79
00:10:10.055 --> 00:10:20.720
<v Maya>He cobbled it together from four things, self-reported sociability in 1981, retrospectively reported sociability at age six, and whether they participated in high school clubs and sports.</v>

80
00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:36.186
<v Daniel>I mean, I have to push back heavily on that. Any skeptic could look at those metrics and say, you aren't measuring a unique economic skill for trading tasks. You are measuring extroversion or self-confidence or just whether someone was a</v>

81
00:10:36.266 --> 00:10:37.527
<v Daniel>popular jock in high school.</v>

82
00:10:38.087 --> 00:10:39.668
<v Maya>That is a very fair critique.</v>

83
00:10:40.028 --> 00:10:54.914
<v Daniel>Right, Deming himself admits this measure might understate the construct, but a critic could argue it's measuring something else entirely. Plus, the 2017 paper is totally silent on whether these coordination skills can actually be taught, or if</v>

84
00:10:55.195 --> 00:11:00.877
<v Daniel>you're just born with them. How did this entire theory survive such a glaring flaw in measurement?</v>

85
00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:13.138
<v Maya>Well, it is the strongest possible objection to the 2017 paper. And the definitive answer to that skeptic didn't arrive until four years later in 2021 from a paper by Ben Whiteman and David Deming.</v>

86
00:11:13.918 --> 00:11:14.398
<v Daniel>What did they do?</v>

87
00:11:15.319 --> 00:11:25.946
<v Maya>They recognized the proxy problem and built a rigorous lab study to prove that the quote unquote team player effect wasn't just a byproduct of extroversion, but a distinct measurable skill.</v>

88
00:11:26.686 --> 00:11:29.328
<v Daniel>How do you isolate a team player in a lab?</v>

89
00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:44.519
<v Maya>You take 255 participants and you constantly scramble them into different teams to solve complex problems. By randomly assigning people to multiple different groups, the researchers could mathematically isolate exactly how much a specific</v>

90
00:11:44.599 --> 00:11:47.241
<v Maya>individual contributed to a team's overall success.</v>

91
00:11:47.481 --> 00:11:49.843
<v Daniel>Entirely separate from their own ability to solve the puzzle.</v>

92
00:11:50.163 --> 00:11:53.285
<v Maya>Completely separate from their own individual ability to solve the puzzle, yes.</v>

93
00:11:53.825 --> 00:12:08.738
<v Daniel>And the numbers they found are stunning. A one standard deviation better team player raises the overall team's performance by 0.13 standard deviations. To put that in context, that boost to the team is about</v>

94
00:12:09.299 --> 00:12:14.984
<v Daniel>65% as large as the effect of the individual's actual raw skill at the task itself.</v>

95
00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:30.504
<v Maya>Being a good collaborator was almost as valuable as being good at the job. Wow. Furthermore, they found this team player effect was not predicted by traditional IQ tests. It was strongly predicted by a psychological assessment called the reading the</v>

96
00:12:30.624 --> 00:12:31.944
<v Maya>mind in the eyes test.</v>

97
00:12:32.484 --> 00:12:34.025
<v Daniel>The RMET.</v>

98
00:12:34.585 --> 00:12:41.427
<v Maya>Where participants literally just look at cropped photos of people's eyes and have to guess what complex emotion the person is feeling.</v>

99
00:12:41.727 --> 00:12:42.107
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

100
00:12:42.577 --> 00:12:53.427
<v Maya>It measures social intelligence and emotion recognition. The people who were good at reading those subtle, unspoken cues were the exact same people who lowered the friction of trading tasks in the randomized groups.</v>

101
00:12:53.507 --> 00:12:54.208
<v Daniel>That's fascinating.</v>

102
00:12:54.628 --> 00:13:00.393
<v Maya>It proved that this is a separate, impactful skill, not just a measure of who was in the debate club in high school.</v>

103
00:13:00.934 --> 00:13:15.816
<v Daniel>Okay. So the theoretical premise holds up. Deming was right. Because computers were historically terrible at human interaction, humans who could coordinate with other humans captured the economic premium. But we have to look closely at a</v>

104
00:13:15.936 --> 00:13:25.523
<v Daniel>very specific assumption Deming made in 2017. He said computers were poor at human interaction, and I'm quoting directly here, so far.</v>

105
00:13:26.290 --> 00:13:30.413
<v Maya>So far, that is the hinge upon which the future of work swings.</v>

106
00:13:30.613 --> 00:13:45.344
<v Daniel>Well, it is now November 2026, and large language models have launched a full-scale attack on the very foundation of Polanyi's paradox, the idea that we know more than we can tell, that tasks resisting explicit rules</v>

107
00:13:45.424 --> 00:13:48.970
<v Daniel>can't be automated. AI doesn't need explicit rules anymore.</v>

108
00:13:49.351 --> 00:13:49.971
<v Maya>It really doesn't.</v>

109
00:13:50.352 --> 00:14:04.509
<v Daniel>It learns from massive patterns. Machines can now navigate nuance, summarize messy meetings, draft highly diplomatic emails, manage cross-functional workflows. So what happens to the social skill premium today?</v>

110
00:14:05.036 --> 00:14:19.506
<v Maya>This brings us to the cutting edge of the debate. There is a draft paper from July 2026 by Lucas Althoff and Hugo Reichardt out of the NBER. They built a dynamic task-based model using that exact same NLSY79 data.</v>

111
00:14:19.946 --> 00:14:20.847
<v Daniel>Okay. What did they find?</v>

112
00:14:21.267 --> 00:14:32.795
<v Maya>First, they confirmed Deming's historical premise. Math and social skills absolutely yielded the highest returns. However, they project that AI is going to fundamentally lower the returns to skill across the board.</v>

113
00:14:33.552 --> 00:14:48.356
<v Daniel>because of a mechanism they call simplification. They talk about AI augmenting workers, sure, but simplification is the real disruptor. AI lowers the cognitive and coordination barrier to complete a task. That means a lower skill worker can suddenly jump in and</v>

114
00:14:48.436 --> 00:14:51.237
<v Daniel>compete for a job that used to be fenced off for a high skill worker.</v>

115
00:14:51.717 --> 00:15:00.600
<v Maya>And when you look at their 2026 model-based forecast, particularly in their moderate AI adoption scenario, the projections for wages and inequality are severe.</v>

116
00:15:01.179 --> 00:15:16.150
<v Daniel>Yeah, let's hit the most sobering numbers from their projection. In the moderate scenario, the 90-10 wage ratio, which measures the gap between the top 10% of earners and the bottom 10%, it falls by 24%. Even if you factor in the massive financial costs of</v>

117
00:15:16.190 --> 00:15:21.795
<v Daniel>companies actually adopting and implementing the AI, that inequality gap still falls by 15%.</v>

118
00:15:22.515 --> 00:15:27.279
<v Maya>Which sounds like a victory for equity until you look at what happens to average wages overall.</v>

119
00:15:27.834 --> 00:15:42.640
<v Daniel>Right. Without adoption costs, the model says average wages rise 28%. But when you factor in the real-world costs of AI adoption, that average wage gain shrinks to a near-stagnant 0.3%. The revolution might</v>

120
00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:46.302
<v Daniel>not make everyone richer. It might just compress everyone into the middle.</v>

121
00:15:47.099 --> 00:16:01.988
<v Maya>We do need to be clear about the limitations here. This is a model-based forecast, not an observed historical effect like Deming's 2017 data. And Althoff and Reichert are relying on nearly the same weak social proxy measures from the original NLSY data. Sure. But</v>

122
00:16:02.008 --> 00:16:06.310
<v Maya>their projections regarding who loses the most ground are incredibly revealing.</v>

123
00:16:06.851 --> 00:16:14.275
<v Daniel>Under their model, verbal skills depreciate the most. Which makes sense. Large language models are, by definition, verbal engines.</v>

124
00:16:15.084 --> 00:16:27.411
<v Maya>Social skill is actually not the biggest loser in their model, but its returns are significantly compressed. They even forecast that college majors intensive in verbal and social skills are going to fare worse in this new AI-integrated landscape.</v>

125
00:16:28.032 --> 00:16:30.073
<v Daniel>Which brings us to a huge crossroads.</v>

126
00:16:30.887 --> 00:16:45.835
<v Maya>If we connect this to the bigger picture, this presses on the ultimate unresolved tension of this entire deep dive. Let's follow Deming's logic to its absolute limit. If social skill is simply the mechanical ability to lower coordination costs, if it is just reducing the friction of</v>

127
00:16:45.895 --> 00:16:50.837
<v Maya>trading tasks, what happens when a machine lowers coordination costs for everyone?</v>

128
00:16:51.238 --> 00:17:01.782
<v Daniel>Exactly. If you no longer need a highly socially skilled project manager to painstakingly translate the marketing team's erratic vision into something the software engineers can actually code.</v>

129
00:17:02.162 --> 00:17:08.465
<v Maya>Because an AI just monitors the Slack channels, synthesizes the requirements and perfectly translates it for both sides.</v>

130
00:17:08.665 --> 00:17:23.595
<v Daniel>Yeah. So what happens to the social premium? Does the premium get erased entirely because the friction is just gone? Or does a massive premium remain for the hardest, messiest, most inherently human parts of dealing with people? Things</v>

131
00:17:23.615 --> 00:17:31.845
<v Daniel>like building actual trust, navigating complex office politics, or showing genuine, unfaked empathy when a client is furious.</v>

132
00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:47.084
<v Maya>As of right now, we have to honestly admit this remains completely unresolved. The economic models point toward compression. The friction of the mundane task trade is disappearing. But the human element, the deep trust required to actually deploy the AI's</v>

133
00:17:47.164 --> 00:17:50.047
<v Maya>output, is notoriously hard to predict or model.</v>

134
00:17:50.813 --> 00:18:05.776
<v Daniel>So what does this all mean? Let's ground this theoretical debate into practical reality. Because if you are analyzing the future of work, especially in the context of higher education, this shifts everything. If social and cognitive complementarity has been</v>

135
00:18:05.796 --> 00:18:14.598
<v Daniel>the key to the labor market for 40 years and is now being aggressively challenged by AI, what exactly should universities be teaching right now?</v>

136
00:18:15.045 --> 00:18:29.931
<v Maya>It creates a logistical nightmare for higher education. Let's think about how a university traditionally credentials a student. They issue a transcript. And a transcript is fundamentally a historical record of individual isolated cognitive achievement.</v>

137
00:18:30.531 --> 00:18:35.533
<v Maya>You sat alone at a desk and got an A on your history paper. You sat alone and got a B on your math exam.</v>

138
00:18:36.231 --> 00:18:47.707
<v Daniel>But the modern labor market doesn't care if you can solve the math problem alone in a silent room. They care if you can trade tasks efficiently with a team while solving the math problem. How do you issue a credential for that?</v>

139
00:18:48.583 --> 00:19:03.433
<v Maya>How do you prove to an employer that a student has the coordination skills that lower friction, especially when the AI is handling the basic translation? It requires a total rethinking of how universities partner with employers. We might need to move away from isolated</v>

140
00:19:03.493 --> 00:19:08.777
<v Maya>lecture halls and toward validating skills in real-world, messy, team-based environments.</v>

141
00:19:09.097 --> 00:19:23.743
<v Daniel>And that brings up a really vital equity question. Think back to how Deming was forced to measure social skills in the historical data, high school clubs, sports, established social networks. I want to be very clear, this is an inference we are drawing based on the</v>

142
00:19:23.803 --> 00:19:38.096
<v Daniel>nature of the proxy metrics, not a specific finding from Deming's paper itself. But if the labor market historically rewards the proxy behaviors of traditional, often privileged social environments, who is most exposed to being left behind?</v>

143
00:19:38.526 --> 00:19:52.369
<v Maya>It is the early career workers. It's the adult learners who didn't have time for after school debate club or varsity sports because they were working a shift to support their family. It is first generation students who might not have had access to those traditional environments where these</v>

144
00:19:52.899 --> 00:19:58.704
<v Maya>specific middle class coordination behaviors are naturally fostered and implicitly rewarded.</v>

145
00:19:58.724 --> 00:20:11.616
<v Daniel>Yeah. If universities can't find a formalized way to teach, measure and credential these skills, they are leaving those marginalized students at a massive disadvantage. The invisible curriculum becomes a barrier to entry.</v>

146
00:20:12.199 --> 00:20:24.505
<v Maya>The era of the isolated genius is over. We know that much. But the era of the human AI hybrid team is just beginning. And the institutions that figure out how to credential that hybrid teamwork will define the next decade of the labor market.</v>

147
00:20:24.885 --> 00:20:39.836
<v Daniel>Which leaves us with a final provocative thought to mull over as we wrap up this deep dive. We started by talking about the flawed linear equation of the future of work, the assumption that technology just demands more raw brainpower. But if AI eventually automates</v>

148
00:20:39.936 --> 00:20:51.203
<v Daniel>all the routine friction of team coordination, if the machine perfectly translates our tasks and manages our workflows, perhaps the most valuable future skill won't be how efficiently you can trade tasks.</v>

149
00:20:51.884 --> 00:20:58.348
<v Maya>Perhaps the ultimate premium will go to cultivating the human judgment to determine which tasks are actually worth trading in the first place.</v>

150
00:20:58.763 --> 00:21:06.454
<v Daniel>A machine can completely optimize the trade, but it still takes a human being to look at the horizon and know what holds real rasting value.</v>

151
00:21:07.075 --> 00:21:09.278
<v Maya>The equation definitely isn't linear anymore.</v>

152
00:21:09.358 --> 00:21:16.949
<v Daniel>Not at all. Thanks for joining us on this deep dive into the hidden engines of the labor market. Keep questioning the variables and we'll catch you next time.</v>
